{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1161295-ctl-and-animism","id":1161295,"title":"CtL and animism","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-11-21T14:58:07-08:00","id":1161295,"title":"CtL and animism","content":"Disclaimer: I've always been a Werewolf player first and foremost, and also have a background in cultural anthropology, so those biases are going to be strongly informing what comes next.<br /> <br /> I'm unsure about how I feel about how Contracts, and Goblin Vows particularly, work in CtL with respect to their encroachment on animist turf- because making bargains (often incorporating bluffing, intimidation, and trickery) is <i>exactly </i>how most real-world animist/shamanic traditions conceptualize what they're doing. A shaman is someone who acts as an ambassador to the spirit world- someone who knows what the spirits want and what they're scared of, and how to leverage those things to make stuff happen (most often in the form of curing a disease or an act of divination).<br /> I realize that in the WoD, the spirit realm is a well-defined and concrete presence, and that there is a difference between making a bargain with a particular death-spirit vs. one with Death as a foundational metaphysical element of reality, or however it is CtL conceptualizes these things (what exactly is Changeling's &quot;Death&quot; that is not the same as &quot;the highest-ranking spirit of death&quot;?). But in terms of themes and so forth, I'm a little uncomfortable with the level of overlap, and I feel it sorta detracts from the core conceit of changeling magic- that stories and oaths are sometimes more powerful than mundane reality.<br /> In terms of Goblin Vows and the like, being able to say &quot;I have a bargain with Waterfalls, so I can make them run backwards if necessary, and in return I have to observe x taboo or complete y task&quot; is squarely within animist territory.<br /> What strikes me as being much more in theme for Changeling are Vows that are tied to narrative elements and inevitability- in other words, Fate. So something like &quot;For as long as I keep my word to never deny hospitality to a fellow wanderer, I will never fail to make my appointed rounds.&quot; The point is that it's tied to an outcome, which becomes &quot;fated&quot; as long as the proper story conditions are met, rather than a dynamic factor in play. So while a Goblin Vow (roads) as written might give you a bonus to travel, a &quot;Fated&quot;-style vow like this would simply make it a <i>certainty</i> that you got to where you were going on time, as long as you observed your conditions. The difference basically comes down to &quot;make helpful things happen right now&quot; vs. &quot;make things turn out a certain way&quot;. It also sets up, IMO, more interesting possibilities for weird stuff to happen- nothing says that you aren't making your appointed rounds with a gun to the back of your head because you've been carjacked by a Loyalist who is forcing you to reveal the locations of all your friends.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/22980-robothedino","name":"robothedino","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-21T15:58:43-08:00","id":1161311,"title":"","content":"Tell me a story about how a man died. What did it mean to him? Was he proud, defiant? Was he weeping? Was he awake? Did he defend himself? Was he done in by a disease? Did he bleed out or freeze to death?<br /> <br /> That's what you're bargaining with. The Shadow has nothing to do with it.<br /> <br /> Negotiating the similarities between Werewolf and Changeling is an interesting exercise, and can be very interesting, but Contracts are not, frankly, a point of overlap. A Changeling's solution is usually in the form of a loophole, whereas Werewolves tend to find ways to cut Gordian knots with a sword.<br /> <br /> Further, the idea of a shaman is not core to either the concept of Werewolves or Changelings. You can do it in both, but you can also do it in Mage, Promethean, and hell, it's not too hard to run with that kind of thing in Vampire, Demon, Mummy, or Beast. No game line owns that turf. Indeed, &quot;shaman&quot; is one of the core conceptual options of a Thyrsus Mage.<br /> <br /> As for your idea of Vows, I'd encourage you to take a look at Pledges. I think that it is entirely reasonable to play Changeling entirely without Contracts, really, and perhaps you'd prefer to. Also, there's a second edition in the works you might want to take a look at and see how much it addresses your concerns.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I call the Integrity-analogue the &quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/822559-trying-to-understand-the-morality-system?p=825008#post825008\" target=\"_blank\">subjective stat</a>&quot;.<br /> An explanation how to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\" target=\"_blank\">use Social Manuevering</a>.<br /> Guanxi Explanations: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=787824#post787824\" target=\"_blank\">1</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/787518-the-mysterium-and-their-contribution-to-mage-society?p=788173#post788173\" target=\"_blank\">2</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/833863-mages-and-trading-favors?p=834531#post834531\" target=\"_blank\">3</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216","name":"Errol216","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-21T16:16:19-08:00","id":1161319,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>robothedino</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1161295-ctl-and-animism#post1161295\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Disclaimer: I've always been a Werewolf player first and foremost, and also have a background in cultural anthropology, so those biases are going to be strongly informing what comes next.<br /> <br /> I'm unsure about how I feel about how Contracts, and Goblin Vows particularly, work in CtL with respect to their encroachment on animist turf- because making bargains (often incorporating bluffing, intimidation, and trickery) is <i>exactly </i>how most real-world animist/shamanic traditions conceptualize what they're doing. A shaman is someone who acts as an ambassador to the spirit world- someone who knows what the spirits want and what they're scared of, and how to leverage those things to make stuff happen (most often in the form of curing a disease or an act of divination).<br /> <br /> I realize that in the WoD, the spirit realm is a well-defined and concrete presence, and that there is a difference between making a bargain with a particular death-spirit vs. one with Death as a foundational metaphysical element of reality, or however it is CtL conceptualizes these things (what exactly is Changeling's &quot;Death&quot; that is not the same as &quot;the highest-ranking spirit of death&quot;?). But in terms of themes and so forth, I'm a little uncomfortable with the level of overlap, and I feel it sorta detracts from the core conceit of changeling magic- that stories and oaths are sometimes more powerful than mundane reality.<br /> <br /> In terms of Goblin Vows and the like, being able to say &quot;I have a bargain with Waterfalls, so I can make them run backwards if necessary, and in return I have to observe x taboo or complete y task&quot; is squarely within animist territory.<br /> <br /> What strikes me as being much more in theme for Changeling are Vows that are tied to narrative elements and inevitability- in other words, Fate. So something like &quot;For as long as I keep my word to never deny hospitality to a fellow wanderer, I will never fail to make my appointed rounds.&quot; The point is that it's tied to an outcome, which becomes &quot;fated&quot; as long as the proper story conditions are met, rather than a dynamic factor in play. So while a Goblin Vow (roads) as written might give you a bonus to travel, a &quot;Fated&quot;-style vow like this would simply make it a <i>certainty</i> that you got to where you were going on time, as long as you observed your conditions. The difference basically comes down to &quot;make helpful things happen right now&quot; vs. &quot;make things turn out a certain way&quot;. It also sets up, IMO, more interesting possibilities for weird stuff to happen- nothing says that you aren't making your appointed rounds with a gun to the back of your head because you've been carjacked by a Loyalist who is forcing you to reveal the locations of all your friends.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div>I sort of watch Changeling from a distance, I have not had a chance to really dig into the new 2e stuff, but I do a lot of work with Folklore and Mythology as graduate work, and I think I might be able to address some of your concerns about these notions. I don't know the extent of your background in Cultural Anthropology, so I'm sorry if I'm repeating any notions for you.<br /> <br /> The notion of making a deal, or pact, or interacting with aspects of reality as Non-Deified Anthropomorphic Personifications is a very common feature of Folklore across the globe. The various systems of Folklore classifications talk about these in different ways IIRC, but some figures are pretty common. Death is the best way to describe these entities, sort of an abstract person who is a) not worshiped b) not honored, c) not venerated, but d) is very much a physical representation of a facet of reality. Some examples of the appearance of Death in Folktales as an individual can be seen <a href=\"http://www.mftd.org/index.php?action=story&amp;id=5385\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, and <a href=\"http://www.mftd.org/index.php?action=story&amp;id=3761\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> if that is at all helpful in case my explanation is unclear. Fate is also often presented in such a way, you can see a collection of Folklore where it is presented in such a way in section N110 in the University of Alberta's online catalog of the Thompson Folklore-Index that you can find <a href=\"https://sites.ualberta.ca/~urban/Projects/English/Motif_Index.htm\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>. I think Old Age shows up as a notable Motif as well, and I'm sure I'm forgetting some better examples of these Entities. Changeling bases its system around Folklore, so these entities play a role in the background of the 'cosmology' for lack of a better term. These are the entities that the Pledges are being formed with, 'Narrative Conceptualizations' or something like that.<br /> <br /> Now, these entities are <i>very different</i> from Abosom, Daemones, Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s, Kami, or other entities dubbed 'animist' entities. At least from what I have been taught, 'Animist' is a concept that's on the way out as it has mostly been used as a dismissive way for the West to address religious groups that were deemed 'less advanced' than others during the Colonial Period. For example, the Daemones would have never been classically depicted as animist entities, but they entirely function as such entities as they were Greek, and thus the 'Right Sort' of religious notion since they were European, and Classical at that. Animist was a term employed to group together religious groups who were very different from each other in a frequently dodgy way, likening them to each other when in reality they were very different from each other. But, these entities are entirely unlike what we see in Folklore. The big difference is simply that the 'Narrative Conceptualizations' are not worshiped, venerated, or respected by individuals. These are not Divine beings, they're not entities that offerings are given to, they're not prayed to, anything like that, they're just characterizations of reality.<br /> <br /> Death from Godfather Death (found <a href=\"http://www.mftd.org/index.php?action=story&amp;id=422\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>) is not Thanatos, or Owu, or other such entities. He is simply Death. Death is not venerated, or given worship, or serve a divine function, it is simply an abstract notion being shaped into a character for the function of the story. This, I feel enforces Changeling's theme that stories and oaths are more powerful than reality at times. These entities are living, breathing Story, not Spirits. They are not animist entities, they are not 'The Spirit of Death,' they just are Death being made into a character. They are fundamentally un-real, forged in story, in ideas. Autumn, Winter, Spring, Summer, entities that are born of the story, not born as the embodiments of reality. They are characterizations of reality, not reality itself.<br /> <br /> I would disagree that a Goblin Vow is squarely within animist territory as what is animist is absolutely fundamentally colossal, unable to be represented with a single term really. The Ashanti would heavily disagree that they can forge pacts with the Abosom in return for favors, or power. They <i>do</i> believe that individuals who serve specific Abosoms will have to follow specific taboos. The Haida <i>do</i> believe specific members of their community can enter relationships with individual Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s, and they <i>do</i> have taboos for these individuals who make contact with the Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s, but it's not related to any deal they make with them, it instead has to do with the cosmological system. One does not make pacts or pledges with Daemones, or follow taboos for them, they just sort of 'do their thing.'<br /> <br /> I would argue that Goblin Vows are a reference to Folkloric (and Mythic) notions that Changeling draws heavily upon. Using our good friend Death again, folkloric concepts such as making a Deal or Bet with Death are something that Goblin Vows seem to be drawing upon rather than an animist concept at all. They might appear to be similar, but they are very different, drawing on different features of human culture entirely.<br /> <br /> I personally <i>love</i> the 'Narrative Conceptualizations' of Changeling. I loved the little fictions of the forging of the Oaths with Summer, Winter, Autumn, and Spring. I liked the notion of there being Iron out there, somewhere. Not really characters for a chronicle, but 'entities' out there as part of Changeling's cosmology. I always loved that they were separate from Spirits, and never explained really. It's something so fundamentally Folkloric in my mind, it's one of the things that draws me to Changeling.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 11-21-2017, 07:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-21T19:48:58-08:00","id":1161395,"title":"","content":"Making deals with the Fair Folk in exchange for boons/power is not something that Changeling made up.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>SWTOR Referal: <a href=\"http://www.swtor.com/r/JQ2nqy\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.swtor.com/r/JQ2nqy</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/414-gallus","name":"Gallus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"886"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-11-22T06:50:43-08:00","id":1161500,"title":"","content":"There are no Goblin Vows in 2e Changeling anymore, so your concern solved itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}